<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eratosthenes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 276–c. 194 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Eratosthenes of Cyrene</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eratosthenes of Cyrene was a Greek scholar who became head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a> under the Ptolemies. He made a remarkably close estimate of the Earth's circumference from the angle of the sun at Syene and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, drew a map of the known world with lines of latitude and longitude, and devised a method for finding prime numbers. His geography, quoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and its peoples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eratosthenes.jpg" alt="Eratosthenes" width="278" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Eratosthenes.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The incense trade carried Sabaean goods and reputation across the Near East and the Mediterranean, and Greek and Roman writers from <b>Eratosthenes</b>, quoted by Strabo, to Pliny the Elder made the Sabaeans a byword for the fabulous wealth of Arabia Felix.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexandria became one of the greatest cities of the ancient world, home to the Library of Alexandria and the Mouseion, which attracted scholars such as Euclid, <b>Eratosthenes</b> and Herophilus, and to the Pharos lighthouse, one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek chronographers, notably <b>Eratosthenes</b>, dated the fall of Troy to 1184 BCE, and later writers placed it in late April.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-24">24 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
